<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Alashiya</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.) · capital Enkomi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Late Bronze Age kingdom on Cyprus whose rulers traded copper with Egypt, the Hittites and Ugarit, as revealed by the Amarna letters.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Alashiya appears in Near Eastern texts from the 18th century BCE as a source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>, and most scholars identify it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> or part of it. Cyprus developed urban centres in the Late Cypriot period from c. 1650 BCE, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enkomi">Enkomi</a> on the east coast, Kalavasos and Alassa, though which was the royal seat is uncertain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The king of Alashiya corresponded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> as a 'brother' in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>, sending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper-ingot">copper ingots</a> in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, oil and luxury goods. Clay analysis shows these letters were made on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enkomi">Enkomi</a> had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> workshops and ashlar buildings, and the island used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypro-minoan-syllabary">Cypro-Minoan</a> script. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> claimed overlordship in the 13th century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Letters from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Ugarit</a> describe enemy ships threatening Alashiya c. 1200 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suppiluliuma-ii">Suppiluliuma II</a> fought naval battles off its coast. In the following century Cypriot towns declined or moved, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> settlers arrived, and the island reorganised into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> kingdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamis-cyprus">Salamis</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-paphos">Paphos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kition">Kition</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-alashiya.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Alashiya" width="320" height="231"><figcaption>Image: Enyavar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Near_East_1400BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 BCE – 1100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Enkomi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Cypriot Bronze Age religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Eteocypriot (probable); Akkadian for diplomacy</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/cyprus">Cyprus</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alashiya, Alasiya</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-1400&amp;focus=kingdom-of-alashiya" aria-label="Kingdom of Alashiya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5527 908 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Alashiya lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5847 1164L5852 1002"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5852 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5847 1164h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5847 1164h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-1400&amp;focus=kingdom-of-alashiya">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

